Barrett's Oesophagus
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Chronic GORD history, Dysphagia, Salmon-pink mucosa on endoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-006
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of retrosternal burning pain with new onset of food sticking in the lower chest, causing panic and inability to swallow solids.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated new-onset dysphagia to solid foods superimposed on a background of chronic gastro-oesophageal reflux disease, accompanied by acute retrosternal distress. Immediate clinical assessment was required to rule out complete oesophageal food bolus impaction requiring emergent endoscopic retrieval versus severe stricture, ensuring airway and nutritional safety prior to the next standard in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My heartburn has been terrible for years, but tonight a piece of meat got completely stuck in my chest and it burns like fire."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 15-year history of chronic gastro-oesophageal reflux disease, partially managed with intermittent over-the-counter antacids. Over the past 4 weeks, noted progressive dysphagia to solid foods, initially bread and meat. Tonight, while eating dinner, experienced sudden, severe retrosternal burning chest pain and the sensation of food lodging in the lower oesophagus. Attempted to clear with water but reports frequent regurgitation of acidic gastric contents and water. Also reports a chronic non-productive night cough.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently, currently pacing due to retrosternal discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. Anxiety related to choking sensation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, holding lower chest, occasionally retching small amounts of clear fluid. No stridor or respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Oropharynx clear, no pooling of saliva. Neck supple, trachea midline. Lungs clear to auscultation bilaterally. Abdomen soft, non-tender, active bowel sounds. Cardiac exam reveals regular tachycardia without murmurs. While endoscopic diagnosis of salmon-pink mucosa is pending, the clinical presentation strongly aligns with a stricture complicating chronic GORD.
Environmental Observations: Antacids and partially eaten meal present on table.
Medication Reconciliation: Reports taking multiple OTC calcium carbonate tablets tonight with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Barrett's Oesophagus - Status: Exacerbated (Suspected stricture with partial food bolus)
Differentials:

Oesophageal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute nature of the current severe sticking sensation following a specific meal, though malignancy remains a significant underlying possibility requiring biopsy.

Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is intimately related to swallowing and food ingestion, with accompanying regurgitation, normal oxygen saturation, and absence of diaphoresis.

Achalasia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Long-standing history of severe acid reflux; achalasia typically presents with dysphagia to both liquids and solids simultaneously without prominent acid reflux.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of long-standing GORD, now complicated by new, pronounced dysphagia and a suspected partial food bolus impaction. The acute inability to tolerate solid foods and ongoing retrosternal distress created a high-risk scenario for complete obstruction or aspiration. Urgent, after-hours medical evaluation was essential to physically assess the patient's airway, administer a trial of oral effervescent agents/muscle relaxants to clear the presumed partial obstruction, and determine if immediate transfer for emergency endoscopy was warranted. Delaying care could have resulted in complete oesophageal obstruction, mucosal ischemia, or aspiration pneumonia.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral effervescent granules (citric acid/simethicone) followed by a small sip of warm water, which resolved the acute sticking sensation. Prescribed Pantoprazole 40mg daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: Elevate the head of your bed by 15-20 cm using solid blocks under the bed frame, not just extra pillows. This uses gravity to physically prevent stomach acid from washing back into your oesophagus while you sleep, preventing further chemical burns and stricture formation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bed risers.
Follow-up: Must see regular GP within 3 days for an urgent gastroenterology referral for an upper endoscopy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you become completely unable to swallow saliva, develop sudden severe chest or back pain, or experience shortness of breath, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The underlying cause of your swallowing difficulty (stricture vs. other pathology) must be visually confirmed by a specialist.
Clinical Course: The acute blockage has cleared, but the narrowing remains. Strict adherence to a soft diet and high-dose acid suppression is required until your scope.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to eat solid foods tonight. If you start drooling because you cannot swallow your own saliva, or if the severe pain returns, you must go to the Emergency Department immediately for a camera test to clear the blockage.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Years of acid reflux have likely caused the lining of your feeding tube to change and scar, creating a narrow ring. The medication provided will strongly suppress acid production to prevent further damage, but it will not stretch the scar tissue.
Actionable Steps: Consume only liquid or very soft, puree-consistency foods for the next 48 hours. Chew any future solid food until it is entirely liquid in your mouth before swallowing to mechanically prevent another piece of food from getting stuck in the narrowed section.

